WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.240
You grip a bar and look at it.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.060
Your eyes say 50 mm.

3
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.660
Your hand says 54.

4
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.700
These numbers are made up, but the question is not.

5
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.440
A nervous system has to decide how much to trust each sense.

6
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.160
Which one do you believe?

7
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.333
Each sense gives a noisy estimate.

8
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:25.693
Suppose the noise in each is independent and Gaussian, the estimates are unbiased,

9
00:00:26.193 --> 00:00:28.353
and you have no prior preference for any height.

10
00:00:29.053 --> 00:00:32.313
Then the combination with the least variance is a weighted average.

11
00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:38.013
Each weight is the sense's precision, 1 over its variance, divided by the sum of the

12
00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:39.013
precisions.

13
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:43.907
Take vision with a standard deviation of 2 mm, and touch with 4.

14
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:47.707
Their variances are 4 and 16 mm².

15
00:00:48.327 --> 00:00:52.407
The weights come out at 0.8 for vision and 0.2 for touch.

16
00:00:53.127 --> 00:00:57.207
The combined estimate is 50.8 mm, much nearer to vision.

17
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.640
The combined variance is 3.2 mm².

18
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.620
The standard deviation is about 1.79 mm.

19
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.060
That is lower than either sense alone.

20
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.520
Touch is the weaker cue, yet it still makes the estimate more precise.

21
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.660
Under these assumptions, a second independent cue always lowers the variance.

22
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:22.953
Now blur the picture until vision has a standard deviation of 8 mm.

23
00:01:23.613 --> 00:01:27.893
The weights swap: touch gets 0.8, and vision 0.2.

24
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:30.893
The estimate moves to 53.2 mm.

25
00:01:31.493 --> 00:01:34.533
The same arithmetic that favoured vision now favours touch.

26
00:01:35.353 --> 00:01:39.493
Alais and Burr found a related pattern for sound and sight in humans: when they

27
00:01:39.493 --> 00:01:43.573
blurred the visual stimulus severely, sound captured the perceived location.

28
00:01:43.573 --> 00:01:43.693
Alay and Burr found a related pattern for sound and vision.

29
00:01:43.693 --> 00:01:44.573
Alay and Burr found a related pattern for sound and vision.

30
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:55.607
Ernst and Banks tested this with four human observers, who judged bar height by

31
00:01:55.607 --> 00:01:58.887
sight and by touch while noise was added to the visual stimulus.

32
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.727
A maximum-likelihood model closely resembled their judgements.

33
00:02:03.347 --> 00:02:06.507
That is one task, four people, and a behavioural fit.

34
00:02:07.107 --> 00:02:09.367
It does not show where in the brain the weighting happens.

35
00:02:10.027 --> 00:02:13.567
Keep the rule: for one event, the more reliable sense weighs more.
